WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.500
"Caffeine Shampoo and&nbsp;
Rosemary Oil for Hair Growth"

00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:09.900
I’ve done videos
on drugs for hair growth,

00:00:09.900 --> 00:00:12.540
supplements for hair growth,
and foods for hair growth.

00:00:12.540 --> 00:00:15.600
What about natural options
that you can apply topically?

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:20.400
I ended my food video on this study,
suggesting consuming pumpkin seed oil

00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:21.900
could increase hair growth.

00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:24.180
What about just rubbing
it on your scalp?

00:00:24.180 --> 00:00:27.480
It works in mice,
but what about men?

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:30.360
Or in this case, women:
pumpkin seed oil

00:00:30.360 --> 00:00:32.820
(about a quarter teaspoon
rubbed onto the scalp once a day)

00:00:32.820 --> 00:00:36.660
was tested head-to-head against
minoxidil foam (5 percent once a day)

00:00:36.660 --> 00:00:40.020
for three months in women
with age-related pattern hair loss.

00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:43.620
Both treatments worked,
but the drug worked better,

00:00:43.620 --> 00:00:45.740
though at about five times the cost.

00:00:45.740 --> 00:00:51.240
A similar experiment comparing a topical
0.2 percent caffeine solution,

00:00:51.240 --> 00:00:54.720
which is about five times stronger
than coffee, with 5 percent minoxidil,

00:00:54.720 --> 00:00:57.600
found they worked similarly well
for balding men,

00:00:57.600 --> 00:01:00.360
though like in the
pumpkin seed oil trial

00:01:00.360 --> 00:01:01.980
there was no third placebo group

00:01:01.980 --> 00:01:05.160
to ensure they all weren’t just
somehow getting better on their own

00:01:05.160 --> 00:01:07.320
(for example due
to seasonal influence,

00:01:07.320 --> 00:01:10.740
with typically greater hair shedding
in the fall than the spring).

00:01:11.340 --> 00:01:14.700
Dripping caffeine on human hair
follicles growing in a petri dish

00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:17.940
enhances hair growth, and indeed,
when finally put to the test

00:01:17.940 --> 00:01:22.560
against placebo won out for both
female and male pattern baldness,

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:25.920
with 85 percent satisfied
after unknowingly using

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:28.260
the caffeine-containing shampoo
for six months,

00:01:28.260 --> 00:01:32.658
compared to only 36 percent
in the placebo shampoo group.

00:01:32.760 --> 00:01:36.600
EGCG, one of the major constituents
of green tea,

00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:41.160
also can promote human hair growth
in vitro and may help balding mice,

00:01:41.160 --> 00:01:44.460
but I failed to find
any green tea clinical trials.

00:01:45.420 --> 00:01:48.360
Pyrithione zinc shampoo,
typically used for dandruff,

00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:52.740
beat out placebo for increasing hair
density in balding men after 26 weeks,

00:01:52.740 --> 00:01:55.680
but not enough for the study subjects
to notice any difference,

00:01:55.680 --> 00:01:58.800
working less than half
as well as the 5 percent minoxidil.

00:01:59.580 --> 00:02:03.420
What about topical herbal treatments
used since time immemorial?

00:02:03.420 --> 00:02:06.900
Ginger offers a good cautionary tale.

00:02:07.620 --> 00:02:11.220
Ginger has a long history
of traditional use in Asia

00:02:11.220 --> 00:02:13.680
to halt hair loss
and heighten hair growth.

00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:17.760
Do a quick search for “ginger shampoo”
and turn up more than a thousand entries.

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:20.220
But when the
Natural Science Foundation of China

00:02:20.220 --> 00:02:23.760
finally put it to the test,
researchers were surprised

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:26.220
it actually
suppressed human hair growth.

00:02:26.220 --> 00:02:28.440
Given their results, they suggested

00:02:28.440 --> 00:02:33.516
ginger could instead be used for
the removal of unwanted body hair.

00:02:33.660 --> 00:02:37.320
Chinese knotweed, known in
traditional Chinese medicine circles

00:02:37.320 --> 00:02:40.680
as He Shou Wu, is a flowering plant
in the buckwheat family

00:02:40.680 --> 00:02:42.660
popularized as a hair tonic.

00:02:42.660 --> 00:02:46.320
Like green tea, there are promising
in vitro and rodent studies,

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:48.060
but no human clinical trials.

00:02:48.060 --> 00:02:52.224
Rosemary, however,
has been put to the test.

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:54.960
One hundred balding men
were randomized

00:02:54.960 --> 00:02:58.440
to twice a day minoxidil
versus a rosemary lotion.

00:02:58.440 --> 00:03:01.140
It took six months,
but eventually there were

00:03:01.140 --> 00:03:05.040
significant comparable improvements
in hair counts in both groups.

00:03:05.040 --> 00:03:08.700
The rosemary lotion appeared to work
as well as the drug.

00:03:08.700 --> 00:03:13.020
You can make it at home by mixing
10 drops of rosemary essential oil

00:03:13.020 --> 00:03:15.120
per fluid ounce
to your favorite lotion

00:03:15.120 --> 00:03:19.140
and then rubbing a quarter teaspoon
onto your scalp twice a day.

00:03:19.140 --> 00:03:24.296
That much rosemary oil would cost
about a penny a week.

